title: Automatic retention
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
aliases: retention log-retention-watcher-entries log-retention-user-entries
summary: The log keeps the last 50 000 entries of the watcher and the last million of everything else; the trim rides the write that triggers it.

A watched repository records every change the watcher sees and would keep them for ever;
most of that history is of no interest. Retention is pruning as a standing policy, counted
in ''entries of the log, per origin'' — an entry being one operation on one or many metarecords ([[Log storage]]): what the log costs is its entries. Two settings, per repository (its `config.json`) or
daemon-wide (`[settings]`, [[Daemon configuration]]):

* ''`log-retention-watcher-entries`'' — how many of the watcher's entries to keep; default 50 000, `0` keeps them all.
* ''`log-retention-user-entries`'' — how many of every other entry to keep: what the user wrote from the command line, the GUI or a script (a trashing included); default 1 000 000, `0` keeps them all.

Two budgets because the volumes are not comparable: a watched tree writes operations all
day, and a limit shared with them would let that churn push the user's own history out.
Nothing in the log depends on an older operation being there — a revert applies onto the
current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]) — so each origin can lose its oldest
operations without making any other one impossible to revert ([[The operation log]]).

A write to many records is one entry, and so are the operations of one context that
followed each other and differ only by their targets ([[Log storage]]): each counts
once and goes whole ([[Mass operations in the log]]).

The limits being on by default, a repository is trimmed on its first write after the
setting takes effect — permanently, without asking. That is the point (an unbounded log is
what it protects against), but a one-way door: a repository whose whole history matters
wants `0` ''before'' it is written again.

The settings before October 2026, `log-retention-revisions` and
`log-retention-keep-labels`, counted revisions and kept labelled ones; they went with the
history tree. The daemon still reads them from `config.toml`, ignores them and says so at
startup; a repository's `config.json` drops them silently the next time it is written.

Until November 2026 the two settings counted operations, as `log-retention-watcher-ops`
and `log-retention-user-ops` (`log_retention_*_ops` in `config.json`). An entry holding
several operations since ([[Log storage]]), they count entries, under the names
above. The shipped `config.toml` brings the new names with the next update of the
configuration; an old name still there is ignored, and the daemon says so at startup,
naming the new one. A repository's `config.json` is read with its old names as the new
ones, and written with the new ones the next time it is written.

!! What a trim does

For each origin past its limit, the trim deletes the oldest entries down to the limit,
with their targets and their keys in the log's indexes ([[Pruning the log]]); a
context left empty stays until a reindex ([[Log contexts]]).
It never reaches into the write that triggered it: a write larger than the limit — a
reconcile of more than a million files — is kept whole, and the log stays past its limit
until the next write trims it. Cut by its own commit, such a write lost its oldest
operations, and undoing it undid only part of it, without a word. The
log keeps the entries of each origin in a table of its own (`log_watcher_entries`,
`log_user_entries`, [[Store tables]]), so
finding the oldest ones reads only what is deleted (`tests/log_cost.rs`). The trim runs
''inside the transaction of the write that triggered it'': cheaper than the append that
triggered it, riding a commit that was being paid for anyway. It does not run at every
write: an origin may drift a tenth of its limit past it (at least one entry) first — so
the oldest, coldest pages are dirtied once per slack, not at every commit.

A trim happens only on a write: a repository nobody writes to keeps what it has.
